<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	What RedwoodTriangle.app is signed with, and why there is anything here at all.

	NOTHING IN THIS PORT NEEDS A SIGNATURE TO RUN. AMFI here is a stand-in that accepts every
	signature and holds no trust cache (libkern/amfi/miniamfi.c), and the kernel is built
	without config_enforce_signed_code, so an unsigned binary executes -- every program in
	/redwood/bin is one. What a signature still buys is ENTITLEMENTS: they are read out of it
	(libkern/amfi/miniamfi_entitlements.cpp) and nowhere else, so a process that wants one has
	to be signed to hold it.

	These three are insurance against the one thing an application has that a /redwood/bin
	program does not: it is started FOR SpringBoard, by RunningBoard, and the daemons on that
	path ask about a bundle's identity and its container. The port has already shown that an
	application with no data container works -- Sileo and TrollStore Helper both launch from
	/Applications here (README) -- and both of those carry a signature of exactly this shape,
	so this is the known-good pairing rather than a guess. If the application turns out to
	launch without them, they cost nothing and can go.

	  application-identifier               what LaunchServices and FrontBoard take a bundle's
	                                       identity from. The same string as CFBundleIdentifier.
	  com.apple.private.security.no-container
	                                       says out loud what is true: there is no data
	                                       container, because there is no installd in this
	                                       recipe to make one. The kernel answers the container
	                                       question from CFFIXED_USER_HOME in the process's own
	                                       environment and a process without it has no
	                                       container (bsd/kern/kern_minimac.c), so this
	                                       entitlement agrees with the kernel rather than
	                                       asking it for anything.
	  platform-application                 the other half of that pairing on a jailbroken
	                                       device. Nothing is confined in this kernel -- the
	                                       Sandbox stand-in allows every query -- so this
	                                       changes no access; it changes what the daemons
	                                       above believe about the process.

	DELIBERATELY ABSENT: anything about the GPU, Metal, IOKit or the display. The driver is
	reached by dlopen of a world-readable bundle and by IOServiceOpen on RedwoodGPU, and
	SpringBoard already does both as mobile with no entitlement of its own for it. An
	entitlement asked for here that was not needed would be a claim about this port that this
	measurement had not made.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>application-identifier</key>
	<string>com.redwood.triangle</string>
	<key>com.apple.private.security.no-container</key>
	<true/>
	<key>platform-application</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
